## Bug Fix Summary

### Root Cause
In `DocumentEditFragment.onResume()`, the method unconditionally called `_hlEditor.setText(_document.getContent())` every time the app returned to the foreground. The `EditText.setText()` method resets the cursor position to the beginning, which is why the cursor always jumped back to position 0 when switching away from and back to the app.

### The Fix
Changed `onResume()` to only call `setText()` when the document content has actually changed:

```diff
     @Override
     public void onResume() {
         super.onResume();
         checkReloadDisk();
-        _hlEditor.setText(_document.getContent());
+        if (!_document.getContent().equals(_hlEditor.getText().toString())) {
+            _hlEditor.setText(_document.getContent());
+        }
     }
```

### Why This Works
- **No disk changes** (normal case): The `checkReloadDisk()` call detects no change and does nothing. The new `equals()` check compares current document content with what's already in the editor — they match — so `setText()` is skipped and the cursor is preserved.
- **Disk has changes** (file modified externally): `checkReloadDisk()` detects the difference and calls `loadDocumentIntoUi()`, which properly saves the cursor position before `setText()` and restores it after. The subsequent `equals()` check will be true (text changed), but `setText()` is called with content that's already in the editor from `loadDocumentIntoUi()`, so it's a no-op.

Note: The project cannot be built due to unavailable network dependencies (jcenter/google), but the fix is a single-line conditional guard in standard Android Java code with no syntax issues.

